[
 [
  "h2",
  "Executive summary"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We will spend around <b>€5.5m</b> recruiting face-to-face agency donors in Germany this year. Over ten years those donors should give us <b>€13.5m</b>, leaving <b>€8.0m in net income</b> after agency fees."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Two things drive what a donor is worth: <b>the monthly amount we ask for</b>, and <b>their age when they sign up</b>. They are not equal, and they do not act separately — age moves value more than the ask does, and it also decides how much a higher ask is worth."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>What we ask for</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Of our main price points, €20 is the best acquisition value on gross and net LTV alike. These donors cost more to acquire than donors giving less, but the larger gift and strong retention more than cover it: <b>€676 net LTV over 10 years against €391 at €10</b>. That holds in every age band from 30 upwards, so it is not simply an effect of €20 happening to attract older donors."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>Donors acquired at €25–€30 return the highest LTV of all, gross and net.</b> However, we have always acquired very few at that level, (under 200 in 2026) so there is less to model from and the figures are indicative. "
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "<b>Agencies could acquire 22–41% fewer donors at €20 than at their current main asks (€5–€15) and we would still be no worse off.</b> That is the trade we should be putting to them."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>Who we ask</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Looking at age rather than gift level, <b>the under 30s are far less profitable compared to the over 30s at every acquisition value</b>. A change in ask doesn't fix the key problem: younger donors do not retain well. This is true of all price points. Under 30s do still remain overall profitable over ten years but we should continue to focus acquisition on the over 30s to generate significant net income, and with them acquire at the highest value possible."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>The €20 fee cap</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The cap at Trust and Activate <b>reduces our agency fees by about €15,000 a year but is costing us more in lost income</b>. Less than 1% of their donors are acquired above €20, ultimately the agencies have no incentive to ask for more: a donor acquired at €20 vs €30 earns them no extra fee, while being worth €473 more to us. We cannot prove the cap is the cause, but Apollon, who have no cap, recruit 4x as many donors above €20 as Activate and 10x as many as Trust."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>How much to trust each price point</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The amount of past data behind each gift level varies enormously, and this is important to consider when reading this analysis. €10 relies on 10,958 past donors, €15 on 8,525 and €20 on 4,513 – we therefore have a high level of confidence when modelling the behaviour of these donors. By contrast, €12–13 relies on 403 donors, €25 on 375 and €30 on 280. We also have little historical data to go on when analysing annual or quarterly donors, so it is important to bear in mind that the margin of error for these amounts is significantly higher."
 ],
 [
  "h2",
  "Methodology"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We take the donors recruited in the first half of 2026, assume the same level of acquisitions in Q3 and Q4, and follow that group for ten years using how donors behaved in 2022–24. Costs come from the 2026 fee schedule, charged donor by donor at the agency’s own rate."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Donors acquired in 2026 are on average, older than the 2022–24 group we model behaviour from - 44 on average against 39, with 18% under 30 rather than 36%. The agencies differ too: Trust and Activate recruit at an average of 47, Apollon and Wesser at 38 and 39. Ignoring that would understate this year’s donors and would credit Trust for being cheap when part of it is simply that they acquire older donors."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We could not match every donor to an agency. We therefore took each agency’s gift value and age profile mix from the donors we could attribute, then scaled up to match the full-year volumes."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "This model looks out to 10 years, Years 1 to 5 come from observed donor behaviour. Years 6 to 10 are projected. The exception to this is annual donors where due to lack of historical data, retention rates are projected from year 1."
 ],
 [
  "h2",
  "The findings"
 ],
 [
  "h3",
  "1",
  "Where we are today"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We expect to acquire 18,898 donors from five agencies this year. 78% are recruited at €15 or below, and 20% of donors are acquired at €20."
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  1,
  "Total projected agency acquisitions in 2026, by gift amount",
  ""
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Note: majority of annual donors are acquired at €60 pa, shown here as €5 per month. A further 55 donors (0.3%) give amounts that do not sit at any of these price points; they are inside every total in this report but are not shown as a group of their own."
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  2,
  "2026 acquisitions and the behaviours we’ve modelled",
  "Volumes and age are 2026 actuals; retention and upgrade rates are historic, from the 2022–24 cohorts."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>2026 acquisitions by agency and gift amount</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Trust and Activate both have €20 fee caps, yet under 1% of their donors are acquired above that, suggesting little motivation for the agency to ask for more."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Trust recruit 32% of their donors at €20, whilst 72% of Activate’s are at €12–13. "
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Wesser recruit mostly at €60 a year, shown here as €5 a month. Their fees are the highest of any agency, but those donors are expected to retain well*, so they still return a positive value after fees."
 ],
 [
  "note",
  "*retention of annual donors is projected from years 1-10. By comparison monthly donors are observed from years 1-5 and projected from years 6-10."
 ],
