 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Trust are the cheapest agency, recruiting the widest spread of amounts and the highest share of donors at €20. Apollon are the most expensive after Wesser. Wesser cost the most per euro of gift and recruit the smallest gifts, but nine in ten of their donors pay annually. We should monitor Wesser donors closely to see if these donors retain better and therefore have a high enough LTV to justify the additional fee."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "It’s worth noting too, that half of our under 30s come from Apollon and a quarter from Wesser – our two most expensive agencies. <b>In summary, we are </b><b>paying more to acquire younger donors who return less.</b>"
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  7,
  "Donor net LTV (10 years)",
  "<b>Important caveat</b><b>.</b> These net LTV figures are across all five agencies, so they carry a <b>blended CPA</b> — the volume-weighted average of what the five agencies charge at each amount. Because the agencies do not spread evenly across the price points, that blend is not identical at every level: €5 is dominated by Wesser at ×29.65 while €20 is mostly Trust at ×19.97."
 ],
 [
  "takeaway",
  "€20 returns €676 over ten years after fees, against €431 at €15 and €391 at €10. €12–13, €25 and €30 each rest on fewer than 500 historic donors and should be read as indicative.",
  "Key Takeaway"
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  8,
  "How long a donor takes to pay for themselves",
  "Months of giving before the donor has covered the agency fee we paid to acquire them. Every amount on the same age profile."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Looking at ROI and breakeven highlights, the lower performance of donors giving €12–13. They take longer to break even than donors giving less (€10). We should monitor the retention rates of these donors closely. They have historically had poor retention rates, though we also have a lower base of these donors. Is there something here about the agency acquiring them? What is it about this giving amount that results in poorer performance?"
 ],
 [
  "h3",
  "5",
  "The fee cap"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The €20 fee cap applies to Trust and Activate. It reduces our agency fees by around €15k a year - a saving on fees, not on income. Today 0.3% of Trust’s donors and 0.9% of Activate’s are recruited above €20. Meanwhile, two of the uncapped agencies manage 3.7% (Apollon) and 2.2% (Wesser). <b>That suggests these agencies holding back recruitment at higher amounts.</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The chart below compares the extra income from a higher gift against the extra fee we would pay for it. The extra income clearly outweighs the extra fee, at Trust and Activate alike."
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  9,
  "What each step above the €20 cap is worth",
  "Each step above €20 for one donor, on that agency’s own age profile and own fee rate. The red bar is the extra fee under the cap, which is always zero; the grey bar is what the fee would be without it.  €25 is left out of this chart. It rests on a small cohort of 375 historic donors whose retention looks unusually low. On equal retention a €25 donor would be worth €178 in extra income."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Can we ask Trust and Activate to test pushing higher gifts with the cap still in place? If the answer is no, because they have no reason to sell at that level, then the cap is what stands between us and the additional income that higher gift amounts would generate."
 ],
 [
  "takeaway",
  "The cap saves around €15,000 a year in fees. However - one Trust donor moved from €20 to €30 is worth €273 in additional net income so the cap only pays for itself if it does not suppress the higher ask. The lack of donors currently recruited at higher levels, suggests the cap is suppressing the higher ask.",
  "Key Takeaway"
 ],
 [
  "h3",
  "6",
  "Scenarios: Acquisition and Retention — what we could gain, and what we could afford to lose"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "This section looks at the trade-off: how much we could afford to lose by asking for more, before it stops being worth it."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>What we could afford to lose: acquisition</b>"
 ],
 [
  "note",
  "<b>Disclaimer before reading:</b> The figures quoted across the acquisition and retention sections here, should be viewed in isolation, e.g. we are saying we could accept a 20% drop in acquisition or a 7pt drop in retention — not both. It also assumes the fee caps stays in place. If fees increased, the numbers may shift though we wouldn’t expect a change in trends."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "For the sake of argument, let’s assume that each agency is going to acquire 1,000 donors at its main ask level, this will be our reference point."
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  10,
  "Donors needed to match the income from 1,000 donors at the main ask",
  "<b>Donors needed to match the €424,724 that 1,000 donors at €10 deliver</b>"
 ],
 [
  "note",
  "Price points where an agency acquires fewer than 1% of its donors are hidden. Each agency is valued on its own age profile and charged its own fee."
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "We can see that for all agencies, we could accept around a 20-40% drop in acquisition before the extra income is no longer worth it. The exact % depends on the increase in ask. For Trust for example, we could allow a drop of 21% if acquiring donors at €15 instead of €10, or a 41% drop if acquiring donors at €20 instead of €10."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>What we could afford to lose: retention</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The same exercise, this time for retention."
 ],
 [
  "figure",
  11,
  "Retention in reserve",
  "How far twelve-month retention on the higher amount could fall before it stopped beating that agency’s main ask on ten-year net value."
 ],
 [
  "takeaway",
  "Moving to €20, Trust could lose 11 points of first-year retention and Activate 10 before the higher ask stopped paying. Apollon has least room at 4 points, as they already ask for €15.",
  "Takeaway"
 ],
 [
  "h3",
  "7",
  "What next"
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>1. Move the ask up, agency by agency</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "€20 returns €676 after fees against €391 at €10, in every age group from 30 upwards, and it is the best of the amounts we sell at scale at all five agencies. It is not necessarily the right ceiling - €30 is worth more everywhere in the model, but we have focused on this less due to the lack of historical data. Treat €20 as the minimum, not the target."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>2</b><b>. </b><b>Remove the fee cap</b><b> </b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "€20+ is where real opportunity sits. Unless agencies are willing to push acquisition at these higher levels with a low fee cap in place, the cap will hold us back. We know that without a fee cap in place donors acquired at a higher level would still generate more net income over their lifetime, but currently agencies are not incentivized to recruit above €20. We should however continue to monitor acquisition trends and retention rates closely."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>3</b><b>. </b><b>Closely monitor </b><b>the retention rates and LTV of annual donors</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "Retention rates of these donors within this report are modelled, not observed. We should closely monitor the performance of annual donors, particularly considering Wesser charges the highest fee across all agencies, and also recruits 25% of all under 30s donors."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>4</b><b>. </b><b>Encourage all agencies to </b><b>recruit</b><b> donors over 30</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "This is already part of the strategy but worth reiterating. Half of every under 30 comes from Apollon at ×24.01, and a further quarter from Wesser at ×29.65. We are paying a lot for donors who don’t retain."
 ],
 [
  "sub",
  "<b>5</b><b>. Tailor the ask to age - and push hardest with the over 30s</b>"
 ],
 [
  "p",
  "The gain from a higher ask rises steeply with age. Moving a donor from €10 to €20 is worth only <b>€29</b> more over ten years if they are under 30, but <b>€309</b> more at 40–49 and <b>€469</b> at 50–64. The same change is sixteen times more valuable with an older donor than a younger one."
 ],
 [
  "h2",
  "Appendix I — Assumptions"
 ],
 [
  "list",
  [
   "2026 volumes. The first half is actual; the second half assumes the same pattern. Volumes are scaled to the realised-donor counts in the cost file.",
   "Behaviour comes from the 2022–24 donors, weighted to this year's age profile. This year's donors average 44 against 39 historically.",
   "Everything is a monthly equivalent. Annual, quarterly and twice-yearly pledges are divided down. This is what the fee is charged on.",
   "Nine gift groups are modelled — €5, €8, €10, €12–13, €15, €20, €25, €30 and a pooled “Other” — so every donor is included.",
   "Confidence varies sharply. €10, €15 and €20 rest on thousands of past donors; €12–13, €25 and €30 on fewer than 500 each, with margins of error of roughly ±21%, ±24% and ±27%.",
   "Upgrades happen in the first year and apply from month 13. Downgrades affect about 1% of donors.",
   "Retention beyond year two is age-specific, using each age group's own observed years 3 to 5.",
   "Years 6 to 10 are projected, not observed. No mortality adjustment is applied, so the 65+ figures are an upper bound.",
   "Scenarios move people, not profiles. Donors moved to a different amount keep that amount's behaviour at their own agency's cost.",
   "Each donor carries their own agency's fee. Costs are not averaged across the five.",
   "The €20 cap applies to Trust and Activate only.",
   "Fee rates come from the 2026 cost file as supplied.",
   "Past behaviour cannot be split by agency. Differences between agencies come from what they charge and who they recruit, not from how their donors behave afterwards.",
   "Acquisition cost only. Development and servicing costs are not included."
  ]
 ],
